{
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "The integral of the conditional density $P(y \\mid x)$ with respect to $y$ results in one, which means $1 = P(x \\mid y) \\cdot P(y)$",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source quote: [00:09:08\u201300:10:00] Now let us integrate both parts with respect to y. What will we obtain in the left-hand part expression? So the integral of conditional density, P of y given x with respect to y. What will it result to? Well, with respect to y, this is just probability density function. And the integral of probability density function always results in one. And this means that we obtain one equals P of x given y times P of y."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The marginal distribution $P(x)$ can be expressed as $P(x) = \\int P(x \\mid y) P(y) \\, dy$",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source quote: [00:10:46\u201300:11:17] U of x U of y times U of y U of y. And this is again quite important expression and it has some clear physical sense. So imagine that you know all conditional distributions on x given arbitrary y's. And now you would like to obtain unconditional distribution on x. So how you can do this? You can simply take this integral and this integral is actually an expectation with respect to y."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The marginal distribution $P(x)$ is the expectation of the conditional distribution $P(x \\mid y)$ over $y$",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source quote: [00:11:17\u201300:11:49] So it is the same as to say that this is expectation with respect to y over all conditional distributions. So we take all different conditional distributions on x given different y's, we average them out with respect to y and we obtain unconditional distribution on x."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The marginalization process for multiple variables can be achieved by integrating out the remaining variables from the joint distribution",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source quote: [00:11:49\u201300:12:44] by induction it's straightforward to prove that this goes through for arbitrary number of random variables so you have say distribution over x and x1 over x1 xn and we would like to get distribution over x1 xk we can easily do it just by taking joint distribution and integrating out remaining variables"
    },
    {
      "claim": "The marginal distribution $P(x)$ is a weighted average of conditional distributions $P(x \\mid y)$ with weights $P(y)$",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source quote: [00:11:17\u201300:11:49] So we take all different conditional distributions on x given different y's, we average them out with respect to y and we obtain unconditional distribution on x."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The phrase \"U of x U of y times U of y U of y\" [00:10:46\u201300:11:17] appears to be a transcription error or unclear speech",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source quote: [00:10:46\u201300:11:17] U of x U of y times U of y U of y. And this is again quite important expression and it has some clear physical sense."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The identity for marginalization is known as [incomplete]",
      "supported": false,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source quote: [00:12:44] this identity is known as"
    }
  ],
  "clarity_1_to_5": 3,
  "caveat": "The ASR has transcription errors and unclear speech that need clarification. The phrase \"U of x U of y times U of y U of y\" is likely a misrepresentation of the joint distribution formula. The identity name is incomplete in the source."
